# 4. Curriculum Design & Spatial-Area Step Scaling Law

Training multi-agent policies directly on full $ 50 \times 50 $ facility layouts leads to exploration collapse due to sparse initial reward signals. HEIST employs a 5-stage geometric curriculum that progressively expands facility dimensions while scaling step budgets proportionally.

## 4.1 Geometric Stage Geometry

The 5 curriculum stages expand map area $ A_k = W_k \times H_k $ smoothly while introducing security entities:

| Stage $ k $ | Map Size $(W \times H)$ | Total Area $ A_k $ | Guards | Cameras | Doors | Primary Learning Objective |
| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |
| **Stage 0** | $ 11 \times 11 $ | 121 tiles | 0 | 0 | 0 | Master basic causal chain (scout $\to$ hack $\to$ extract) |
| **Stage 1** | $ 17 \times 17 $ | 289 tiles | 1 | 0 | 1 | Master 1-guard patrol timing |
| **Stage 2** | $ 25 \times 25 $ | 625 tiles | 2 | 1 | 2 | Master single-camera vision cone evasion & hacking |
| **Stage 3** | $ 35 \times 35 $ | 1,225 tiles | 3 | 2 | 3 | Master multi-camera security coordination |
| **Stage 4** | $ 50 \times 50 $ | 2,500 tiles | 4 | 3 | 4 | Full benchmark facility extraction |

## 4.2 Formalization of Spatial Sample Invariance ($ \rho^* $)

In a 2D Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (Dec-POMDP) with local vision window size $ w_{\text{obs}} \times h_{\text{obs}} $, the spatial sample density $ \rho $ is defined as the expected number of environment transition steps collected per unit grid area:

$ \rho(T_k, A_k) = \frac{T_k}{A_k} \quad \left[ \frac{\text{environment steps}}{\text{grid tile}} \right] $

### Empirical Derivation of Optimal Sample Density ($ \rho^* $)

Analyzing empirical training rollouts on Stage 0 ($ A_0 = 121 \text{ tiles} $), policy returns for multi-agent policy gradient algorithms stabilize and reach convergence plateau at $ T_{\text{conv}} \approx 120,000 \text{ steps} $.

This establishes the empirical optimal spatial sample density $ \rho^* $:

$ \rho^* = \frac{120,000 \text{ steps}}{121 \text{ tiles}} \approx 991.74 \text{ steps / tile} \approx \mathbf{1,000 \text{ steps / tile}} $

To achieve **Spatial Sample Invariance** across all curriculum stages—ensuring policy gradient estimators (GAE) receive an identical density of observations per tile regardless of facility size—we require $ \rho $ to remain anchored at $ \rho^* \approx 1,000 \text{ steps/tile} $:

$ \rho(T_k, A_k) = \rho^* \quad \forall k \in \{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\} $

## 4.3 Parameterizable Step Scaling Law Formula

Equating spatial sample densities $ \frac{T_k}{A_k} = \rho^* $ yields the exact step scaling law used by `scripts/train.zsh`:

$ T_k = T_0^* \cdot \frac{W_k \cdot H_k}{121} $

where $ T_0^* = 120,000 \text{ steps} $ is the empirically grounded Stage 0 baseline step budget.

### Empirically Grounded Multi-Stage Step Allocation ($ T_0^* = 120,000 $)

Using the grounded Stage 0 baseline $ T_0^* = 120,000 \text{ steps} $, the exact multi-stage step allocations across the 5 curriculum stages are:

| Curriculum Stage $ k $ | Facility Area $ A_k $ | Area Scale Factor $ \frac{A_k}{121} $ | Grounded Step Budget ($ T_k $) | Cumulative Campaign Steps |
| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| **Stage 0** ($ 11 \times 11 $) | 121 | $ 1.000\times $ | **120,000 steps** (120k) | 120,000 steps |
| **Stage 1** ($ 17 \times 17 $) | 289 | $ 2.388\times $ | **286,611 steps** (287k) | 406,611 steps |
| **Stage 2** ($ 25 \times 25 $) | 625 | $ 5.165\times $ | **619,834 steps** (620k) | 1,026,445 steps |
| **Stage 3** ($ 35 \times 35 $) | 1,225 | $ 10.124\times $ | **1,214,876 steps** (1.21M) | 2,241,321 steps |
| **Stage 4** ($ 50 \times 50 $) | 2,500 | $ 20.661\times $ | **2,479,338 steps** (2.48M) | 4,720,661 steps |
| **Total Campaign** | — | **$ 39.338\times $** | **4,720,661 steps** (4.72M) | — |

This spatial scaling law mathematically guarantees that every curriculum stage maintains the exact same spatial sample coverage $ \rho^* \approx 1,000 \text{ steps / tile} $, providing a rigorous, empirical foundation for multi-stage MARL evaluation.
